  <h1>Types of Age Discrimination</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Riley Chen (RC), Employment &amp; Civil Rights Practice Lead. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>The but-for causation standard</h2>
  <p>The Supreme Court held in <em>Gross v. FBL Financial Services</em> (2009) that ADEA plaintiffs must prove age was the &ldquo;but-for&rdquo; cause of the adverse action &mdash; a higher standard than Title VII&rsquo;s mixed-motive framework. This means the plaintiff must show that absent age discrimination, the adverse action would not have occurred. Mixed-motive defenses (the employer had both legitimate and illegitimate reasons) are not available to ADEA defendants in the same way they are under Title VII.</p>
  <h2>Disparate treatment</h2>
  <p>Direct or circumstantial evidence that the employer treated the employee differently because of age. Circumstantial evidence includes: statistical evidence (the employer consistently fired workers over 55 while retaining younger employees), comparator evidence (younger, less qualified employees were kept), and stray remarks (age-related comments by decision-makers, even if not directly linked to the adverse decision).</p>
  <h2>Disparate impact</h2>
  <p>A neutral policy with disproportionate adverse effect on workers 40+. The employer can defend by showing the practice was justified by &ldquo;reasonable factors other than age&rdquo; (RFOA). Common disparate impact claims involve workforce reductions that disproportionately eliminate older workers, educational requirements that older workers are less likely to meet, and algorithmic hiring tools that serve as age proxies.</p>
  <h2>Harassment</h2>
  <p>A hostile work environment based on age. Must be objectively severe or pervasive enough to alter working conditions. Isolated age-related jokes rarely qualify; a sustained campaign of age-based ridicule, exclusion, or demeaning treatment can. The employer is liable if it knew or should have known and failed to correct.</p>
  <h2>Retaliation</h2>
  <p>Protected activity under the ADEA includes filing an EEOC charge, making an internal complaint, or participating in an ADEA investigation. Adverse action following protected activity (especially close in time) raises a retaliation inference. Retaliation claims have a separate, more plaintiff-favorable causation standard than the but-for standard for age discrimination itself.</p>
